#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SESSION_NOTES-S248-through-S242.md.verify.sh — the losslessness proof for this shard.
#
# Generated by Session 256 (the NINTH trim). Re-runnable, read-only, exits non-zero on failure.
#
#   bash <this>              prove the ninth trim was lossless AND landed where it was declared
#   bash <this> --self-test  prove this proof can FAIL (108 mutants; exit 2 if any survives)
#
# Descended from Session 245's SESSION_NOTES-S241-through-S239.md.verify.sh, itself descended from
# Session 242's, 239's, 235's, 231's, 228's, 224's and 222's — read those for the rationale behind
# L0-L3, L4, L5-L7, L8-L9, L10-L11, L12, L13 and L14. Shards are WRITE-ONCE: this proof pins ITS
# OWN cut key and does not re-prove the earlier shards' cuts. Run all nine shard proofs, and both
# collapse proofs, which own the front-matter table this cut adds a row to.
#
# NO ASSERTION IS ADDED HERE. The operator's ruling F (docs/planning/ledger-budgets-review.md
# §13.1, 2026-09-07) ends the assertion-per-trim convention: a trim adds an assertion only on
# evidence of a real gap, and the CI step running `--self-test` on every proof is the substitute
# the ruling named.
# The `L15` bequeathed to this trim — a FILE CENSUS of the trim's own sweep — is therefore not built;
# the operator confirmed in Session 256 that the ruling covers it. The sweep is published instead,
# as a dated fact, in Session 256's close-out record — the commit after this one.
#
# WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY EACH CHANGE IS A RE-TARGET RATHER THAN A WEAKENING.
#
# Two rulings retired the prose this lineage used to read. Session 254 (Option E, retroactive)
# replaced the live file's prose POINTER BLOCKS with one table and made collapse-on-write the rule:
# a trim adds a ROW, and the R-series' R8 goes red if prose comes back. Session 255 (Option D,
# widened) replaced the line-count retention rule with a byte rule. Every inherited operand that
# read either was found the way CLAUDE.md prescribes — the S241 proof's declarations run against the
# working tree at bf776a9, each literal present at its trim commit but absent now flagged (23 of
# 101) — and each is re-targeted to the artifact that replaced it. None is dropped unreplaced:
#
#   L2   the declared POINTER block  ->  ROW, the one table row this trim inserts. L2/b1 still proves
#        the front matter changed by exactly one insertion plus the declared substitutions.
#   L5   the pointer block's routing clauses  ->  the front-matter TABLE: each row's `archived` span
#        and `shard` name, plus the frontier (newest archived + 1 -> the live file). L5 still reads
#        three copies — the table, the shard banner, and CLAUDE.md's routing clauses.
#   L11  1,500 / 1,050 / 4 LINES  ->  fire above 196,608 B, land at or under 98,304 B, keep at least
#        4 NON-STUB records, each held against the sentence in CLAUDE.md that declares it.
#        REWRITTEN, not re-tuned. The floor counts non-stub records with a FROZEN copy of
#        tests/test_read_budget.py's classifier (at 3a141ec): the guard's copy may change, and this
#        proof's meaning must not change with it.
#   L12  the pointer block's size figures  ->  L12/row, which COMPOSES this trim's table row from
#        the artifacts — ordinal, trim session, both spans, record count, archived lines, shard name,
#        shard total and the assertions added — and requires it to equal ROW. The row cannot name
#        the commit it is written in, so its trim cell says `this commit`, as the table's legend
#        now declares. The `read_cap` (2,000 LINES, a premise measured false in Session 249) ->
#        `refuse_bytes` (262,144): L12/cap fails if a default Read would REFUSE the shard, which is
#        the claim its banner makes. The banner states no byte count on purpose: every mutant that
#        edits the banner would move it, and L12 would steal every one of them.
#   L13  the pointer block's span sentences  ->  L12/row's two span cells. The CENSUS sentence moves
#        to the shard BANNER, which L6 pins byte-for-byte; collapse-on-write leaves it no home in
#        the front matter.
#   L14  its census sentence -> the banner too. The eighth shard's figures JOIN its literals — five
#        of them, one BACKLOG chain widened — which is the handover L14/set exists to force.
#   L8   REACH re-declared for nine shards. Three inherited required strings were already retired
#        at HEAD: two by Session 249, and `Copy all of L0–L14 forward`, which survived only as a
#        QUOTATION inside CLAUDE.md's own list of retired operands — so an inherited L8/required
#        would have passed on the quote.
#   L10  gains the S241 proof, frozen at 4ab6306: its only commit, measured rather than assumed. One
#        message string lost a count word ("seven banners") instead of gaining another.
#
# FOUR INHERITED ARMS ARE STRENGTHENED, never relaxed, each on an adversarial review's evidence and
# each with a mutant the old form let through. L12/prose, L13/span and L14's span and size arms
# match a figure only on digit or word boundaries — "11,681" passed where "1,681" was declared,
# "seventeen" where "seven" was (M102-M104, M107). Both census arms parse their sentence by
# position — a swapped L14 census and "twenty-five" for "five" passed (M106, M108). And two arms
# are NEW inside inherited assertions: L2/position, because a row moved out of the table passed
# every check (M105); and L12/row's unreadable-proof arm, because an unreadable proof composed an
# `added` cell of "none" and matched (M101).
#
# The fifth trim narrowed L2/b3 to the banner on a condition: TRANSFORM is None and L1 runs. Both
# hold here (a pure byte slice; L1 in check()), so the narrowed form is carried unchanged.
#
# MEASURED COVERAGE — produced by running both loops against FROZEN artifacts, the exit code checked on
# every run: 0 or 2, never anything else, which would be a broken run scored as a clean one
# (learning #190). The harness is described in Session 256's close-out record.
#
#   WHOLE ASSERTION — neuter `fails += Ln(...)` to `pass`; what survives is what Ln ALONE catches:
#
#     L0 (none)   L1 (none)*   L2 M10-M15,M40,M51,M68,M98-M100,M105   L3 (none)   L4 (none)
#     L5 M18-M23,M25,M26,M38,M39,M47,M52   L6 M27   L7 M29,M30   L8 M31-M34,M41,M43-M46,M50
#     L9 M35-M37   L10 M53-M55   L11 M56-M59,M84   L12 M60-M67,M73,M81,M82,M96,M97,M101,M102,M107
#     L13 M69-M72,M74-M80,M83,M85,M103,M108   L14 M86-M95,M104,M106
#
#     * L1's blank entry is NOT a licence to remove it. L3 and L4 catch everything L1 catches, but
#       L1 is what the fifth trim's narrowing of L2/b3 rests on. Read '(none)' as 'overlapped'.
#
#   PER ARM — 68 failure-emitting statements in L0-L14 (`out.append(...)` to `pass`, a non-empty
#   `return [...]` to `return []`, type-preserving — learning #191). 48 of them uniquely catch a
#   mutant. A first sweep found L2/b0, L2_ROW_MISSING and L2/b1 reachable only in company, and M98,
#   M99 and M100 were built to isolate them. The 20 still without a sole catcher, by cause:
#
#   * OVERLAPPED BY INHERITANCE (7) — L0's trailing-block arm, L1, L3's three and L4's two. Each
#     fires on the mutants aimed at it; the derivation arms (L12/row, L13) fire alongside. A cut
#     shifted with EVERY declaration moved to match would be L4's alone; building it re-declares
#     the whole proof, and is recorded rather than built.
#   * PROVABLY SUBSUMED (10) — L2/b2 (a vanished line always changes the residue b1 compares, and
#     with the row missing b1 yields to L2_ROW_MISSING); L5/2's frontier arm and both L5/3 arms (the
#     table copy derives its frontier as newest archived + 1, and this shard's clause cannot move
#     without its row, which L12/row composes from the records); the four UNREADABLE arms of L8,
#     L12, L13 and L14 (no file is read by one assertion alone); L9's and L14/blob's no-add-commit
#     arms (a shard with no add-commit is unmeasurable to both).
#   * VACUITY GUARDS (3), never meant to fire alone — L0's no-records arm, L5/4's no-numeric-ids arm
#     and L13's empty-records-zone arm.
#
# The grammar is FROZEN into this file on purpose: a later change to the trim design must not
# silently change what THIS shard's proof means.
